Implementation detail: `Expr.getUsedConstants` returns Array Name. Traverse:

```lean
partial def closure (env : Environment) (seen : Array Name) (todo : List Name) : Array Name :=
  match todo with
  | [] => seen
  | c :: rest =>
    if seen.contains c then closure env seen rest
    else
      let seen := seen.push c
      let next : List Name :=
        match env.find? c with
        | none => []
        | some ci =>
          let t := ci.type.getUsedConstants.toList
          let v := match ci.value? with | some e => e.getUsedConstants.toList | none => []
          (t ++ v).filter (fun n => !seen.contains n)
      closure env seen (next ++ rest)
```

Careful: `env.find?` returns Option ConstantInfo. Also, the closure could be huge (mathlib). For each pair, computing closure of d might traverse tens of thousands of constants; 40 pairs → fine but slow-ish. Alternative: stop early when target found. Let me add an early-exit version: `reaches (env) (target) (todo) (seen)` returning Bool.

```lean
partial def reaches (env : Environment) (target : Name) (seen : Array Name) (todo : List Name) : Bool :=
  match todo with
  | [] => false
  | c :: rest =>
    if c == target then true
    else if seen.contains c then reaches env target seen rest
    else
      let seen := seen.push c
      let next := ...
      reaches env target seen (next ++ rest)
```
This terminates when found. For non-found cases it traverses the full closure (could be big: a theorem's proof closure can include thousands of mathlib lemmas; fine).

